{"name":"Deep Atlantic Blue","hex":"#004f57","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deep","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Extending, reaching or positioned far from a point of reference, especially downwards.\nExtending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively.","Extending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Far, especially far down through something or into something, physically or figuratively.","In a profound, not superficial, manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deep"},{"term":"atlantic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the atlas, the uppermost vertebra in the spinal columns of humans and some other animals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/atlantic"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Atlantic Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The Atlantic Ocean is the second largest of the world's five oceans, with an area of about 85,133,000 square kilometers (32,870,000 sq mi). It covers approximately 17% of Earth's surface and about 24% of its water surface area. During the Age of Discovery, it was known for separating the New World of the Americas from the Old World of Afro-Eurasia."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/Atlantic_Ocean_-_en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atlantic_Ocean","wikidata":"Q97"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Kill_pier%2C_Clare_Island%2C_Co_Mayo_02.jpg/500px-Kill_pier%2C_Clare_Island%2C_Co_Mayo_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Kill_pier%2C_Clare_Island%2C_Co_Mayo_02.jpg/1280px-Kill_pier%2C_Clare_Island%2C_Co_Mayo_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Kill_pier%2C_Clare_Island%2C_Co_Mayo_02.jpg/500px-Kill_pier%2C_Clare_Island%2C_Co_Mayo_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Atlantic as seen from the shores of Clare Island, Ireland, in 1981","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKill_pier%2C_Clare_Island%2C_Co_Mayo_02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Atlantic_bathymetry.jpg/500px-Atlantic_bathymetry.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.o